; DE-BRUIJN INDEX discriminator: the 3rd arg back-refs the FIRST var, not the most
; recently pushed one. A `references` table indexed by push-order rather than e.v
; matches (d _ $y $y) instead and yields (out 1 2)->(out 1 2)? no: it yields the
; WRONG set (e.g. (out 5 6) below instead of (out 1 2)/(out 3 4)).
(d 1 2 1)
(d 1 2 2)
(d 3 4 3)
(d 5 6 6)
(exec 0 (, (d $x $y $x)) (, (out $x $y)))
